WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.035
Imagine a Torrance agenda item proposing a neighborhood resilience and mobility center on city-owned land.

2
00:00:07.035 --> 00:00:17.587
The concept combines a shaded transit stop, emergency supply space, community rooms, bicycle facilities, a small commercial kiosk, and stormwater improvements.

3
00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:20.109
A grant may fund part of construction.

4
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:22.365
A partner may operate programming.

5
00:00:22.365 --> 00:00:24.290
Supporters see many benefits.

6
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.480
Skeptics see cost and complexity.

7
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.798
Your capstone task is not to advocate immediately.

8
00:00:29.798 --> 00:00:39.620
It is to build a decision record that lets the public and officials see purpose, alternatives, full cost, revenue, risk, equity, and accountability.

9
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:51.064
This episode is educational preparation, not financial, legal, accounting, engineering, environmental, planning, real estate, tax, investment, or municipal advisory advice.

10
00:00:51.064 --> 00:00:52.994
The capstone is hypothetical.

11
00:00:52.994 --> 00:01:04.239
For an actual Torrance project, use current official documents and qualified city staff, counsel, auditors, engineers, planners, appraisers, and financial professionals.

12
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.301
Step one is frame the decision.

13
00:01:06.301 --> 00:01:09.628
Write a need statement without naming the project.

14
00:01:09.628 --> 00:01:19.808
For example: provide reliable neighborhood space for daily mobility, community use, and emergency support while improving heat and stormwater resilience.

15
00:01:19.808 --> 00:01:22.736
Then list required outcomes and constraints.

16
00:01:22.736 --> 00:01:25.397
Identify decision authority and process.

17
00:01:25.397 --> 00:01:26.395
What body acts?

18
00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:33.648
What planning, environmental, procurement, property, funding, rate, or contracting approvals may be required?

19
00:01:33.648 --> 00:01:35.844
Mark legal questions for counsel.

20
00:01:35.844 --> 00:01:40.102
Do not treat the capstone as a substitute for required analysis.

21
00:01:40.102 --> 00:01:42.165
Step two is establish evidence.

22
00:01:42.165 --> 00:01:43.828
Create a source register.

23
00:01:43.828 --> 00:01:58.200
For Torrance facts, look to current operating budgets, Capital Improvement Plans, budget reviews, ACFRs, public agenda reports, project dashboards, adopted plans, fee schedules, contracts, and other official records.

24
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.995
Add county or state sources when relevant.

25
00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:05.054
Record title, date, page, link, and what the source supports.

26
00:02:05.054 --> 00:02:08.913
Distinguish fact, estimate, assumption, and policy choice.

27
00:02:08.913 --> 00:02:11.042
Facts are documented conditions.

28
00:02:11.042 --> 00:02:13.437
Estimates apply methods to evidence.

29
00:02:13.437 --> 00:02:16.298
Assumptions fill uncertainty transparently.

30
00:02:16.298 --> 00:02:18.561
Policy choices express priorities.

31
00:02:18.561 --> 00:02:21.621
Do not let one category masquerade as another.

32
00:02:21.621 --> 00:02:23.875
Step three is define alternatives.

33
00:02:23.875 --> 00:02:31.830
Include the proposed combined center, a smaller phased version, separate improvements at existing sites, and a baseline.

34
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:37.465
Screen each for authority, feasibility, timing, and ability to meet minimum outcomes.

35
00:02:37.465 --> 00:02:39.653
Step four is map life-cycle cost.

36
00:02:39.653 --> 00:02:58.148
Include land opportunity cost, design, engagement, environmental work, construction, equipment, grants administration, staffing, utilities, cleaning, security, programming, information technology, insurance, maintenance, renewal, contract oversight, and eventual decommissioning.

37
00:02:58.148 --> 00:03:01.331
Assign owner, timing, range, and source to each.

38
00:03:01.331 --> 00:03:02.988
Create an asset register.

39
00:03:02.988 --> 00:03:13.926
Include shelter, pavement, drainage, landscaping, lighting, cameras if proposed, bicycle equipment, restroom systems if proposed, furnishings, kiosk, and technology.

40
00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:17.042
Give each a maintenance owner and renewal year.

41
00:03:17.042 --> 00:03:18.832
Step five is map resources.

42
00:03:18.832 --> 00:03:31.228
List grant proceeds, General Fund support, restricted funds, service charges, rent, partner contributions, financing, and developer-related sources only if legally and factually relevant.

43
00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:37.659
Label each one-time or ongoing, restricted or flexible, confirmed or uncertain, and gross or net.

44
00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:45.150
Do not count the grant until conditions, match, reimbursement timing, eligible cost, and deadline are understood.

45
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.730
Do not count rent without vacancy and management cost.

46
00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.719
Do not call borrowing revenue.

47
00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:55.226
Do not assume a restricted balance can support unrelated operations.

48
00:03:55.226 --> 00:03:57.421
Step six is assess fiscal impact.

49
00:03:57.421 --> 00:04:00.015
Build a baseline and incremental model.

50
00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:04.272
If the city-owned site currently has a use or value, include it.

51
00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:09.128
Separate construction-period activity from stabilized annual performance.

52
00:04:09.128 --> 00:04:11.323
Show effects by fund and by year.

53
00:04:11.323 --> 00:04:19.571
For any property-tax claim, use the city's applicable share and authoritative allocation information, not the full tax bill.

54
00:04:19.571 --> 00:04:25.623
For retail revenue, test whether spending is new or shifted from another Torrance business.

55
00:04:25.623 --> 00:04:31.077
For service cost, ask departments about marginal workload and capacity thresholds.

56
00:04:31.077 --> 00:04:33.472
Step seven is evaluate public value.

57
00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:44.779
Score service quality, safety, access, equity, climate resilience, plan consistency, neighborhood effect, economic opportunity, deliverability, and fiscal sustainability.

58
00:04:44.779 --> 00:04:46.908
Publish definitions and weights.

59
00:04:46.908 --> 00:04:49.502
Add confidence ratings and sensitivity.

60
00:04:49.502 --> 00:04:51.364
Step eight is test delivery.

61
00:04:51.364 --> 00:04:58.016
Compare direct city operation, a nonprofit or public partner, and a private contract where feasible.

62
00:04:58.016 --> 00:05:05.598
Define outputs and allocate design, construction, demand, operating, regulatory, technology, and continuity risks.

63
00:05:05.598 --> 00:05:10.121
Include oversight, data, audit, remedies, termination, and handback.

64
00:05:10.121 --> 00:05:11.984
Step nine is test financing.

65
00:05:11.984 --> 00:05:17.105
Compare pay-as-you-go, phasing, grants, and any lawful borrowing alternative.

66
00:05:17.105 --> 00:05:26.550
If debt is considered, match term to asset life, show total repayment, identify security, model stress, and disclose long-term administration.

67
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:30.341
Financing should follow the service and project strategy.

68
00:05:30.341 --> 00:05:32.004
Step ten is set reserves.

69
00:05:32.004 --> 00:05:38.589
Identify construction contingency, operating stabilization, emergency, and asset replacement needs.

70
00:05:38.589 --> 00:05:42.048
Avoid using one reserve label for every uncertainty.

71
00:05:42.048 --> 00:05:44.509
Show permitted use and replenishment.

72
00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:45.708
Now run scenarios.

73
00:05:45.708 --> 00:05:49.304
The expected case uses documented central assumptions.

74
00:05:49.304 --> 00:05:55.698
The downside case delays the grant reimbursement and kiosk opening while raising operating cost.

75
00:05:55.698 --> 00:06:01.359
The stress case combines construction escalation, lower revenue, and partner failure.

76
00:06:01.359 --> 00:06:05.089
For each, identify actions, owners, and decision points.

77
00:06:05.089 --> 00:06:06.754
Conduct an equity review.

78
00:06:06.754 --> 00:06:08.019
Who benefits daily?

79
00:06:08.019 --> 00:06:09.884
Who benefits in emergencies?

80
00:06:09.884 --> 00:06:11.216
Who travels farther?

81
00:06:11.216 --> 00:06:12.348
Who pays charges?

82
00:06:12.348 --> 00:06:15.478
Who bears construction or displacement effects?

83
00:06:15.478 --> 00:06:18.409
What accessibility and language needs exist?

84
00:06:18.409 --> 00:06:23.071
Could targeted assistance preserve access without obscuring full cost?

85
00:06:23.071 --> 00:06:24.469
Conduct a pre-mortem.

86
00:06:24.469 --> 00:06:28.332
Imagine the center is struggling five years after opening.

87
00:06:28.332 --> 00:06:39.255
Possible causes include ambiguous ownership, weak programming demand, high cleaning cost, failed equipment, vacancy, grant noncompliance, or an inflexible contract.

88
00:06:39.255 --> 00:06:43.917
Convert each plausible cause into mitigation or an approval condition.

89
00:06:43.917 --> 00:06:45.915
Then prepare a recommendation.

90
00:06:45.915 --> 00:06:52.375
Options include proceed, proceed with conditions, phase, redesign, seek more evidence, or reject.

91
00:06:52.375 --> 00:06:59.235
A conditional approval should name conditions, deadlines, owners, and what happens if they are not met.

92
00:06:59.235 --> 00:07:02.299
Create a monitoring dashboard before approval.

93
00:07:02.299 --> 00:07:16.285
Include schedule, capital budget, grant status, operating forecast, reserve level, asset readiness, partner performance, usage, service quality, incidents, revenue, maintenance work orders, and equity measures.

94
00:07:16.285 --> 00:07:20.148
Set reporting frequency and thresholds for council review.

95
00:07:20.148 --> 00:07:24.332
Your learner artifact is the Capstone Fiscal Evaluation Packet.

96
00:07:24.332 --> 00:07:27.454
Section one: one-page executive decision brief.

97
00:07:27.454 --> 00:07:32.037
Section two: need, outcomes, authority, process, and source register.

98
00:07:32.037 --> 00:07:35.757
Section three: baseline and at least three alternatives.

99
00:07:35.757 --> 00:07:41.004
Section four: life-cycle cost, asset register, and ten-year operating forecast.

100
00:07:41.004 --> 00:07:44.458
Section five: revenue authority and reliability map.

101
00:07:44.458 --> 00:07:48.244
Section six: incremental fiscal impact by fund and phase.

102
00:07:48.244 --> 00:07:52.694
Section seven: public-value matrix, equity review, and sensitivity.

103
00:07:52.694 --> 00:07:57.742
Section eight: delivery, partnership, financing, debt, and reserve analysis.

104
00:07:57.742 --> 00:08:01.395
Section nine: expected, downside, and stress scenarios.

105
00:08:01.395 --> 00:08:06.642
Section ten: recommendation, approval conditions, monitoring, and review dates.

106
00:08:06.642 --> 00:08:08.834
Use no invented Torrance figures.

107
00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:20.325
If official information is unavailable, write not established, provide a hypothetical range clearly labeled as illustrative, or identify the responsible office and question.

108
00:08:20.325 --> 00:08:22.185
Now, three recall questions.

109
00:08:22.185 --> 00:08:26.834
Question one: What four labels prevent confusion in the evidence base?

110
00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:30.421
Answer: Fact, estimate, assumption, and policy choice.

111
00:08:30.421 --> 00:08:35.801
Question two: Why must the capstone include a baseline and multiple alternatives?

112
00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:47.292
Answer: Because fiscal and public value are incremental, and a fair decision requires comparison with what happens without the proposal and with other ways to meet the need.

113
00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:51.344
Question three: What makes a conditional approval meaningful?

114
00:08:51.344 --> 00:09:00.443
Answer: Specific conditions, owners, deadlines, evidence requirements, monitoring thresholds, and consequences if conditions are not met.

115
00:09:00.443 --> 00:09:12.774
This capstone was organized with reference to the public nine-module outline for the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy course Foundations of Local Government Finance in the United States.

116
00:09:12.774 --> 00:09:15.359
The outline guided topic coverage only.

117
00:09:15.359 --> 00:09:19.933
No account-gated Lincoln lesson was accessed or claimed as extracted.

118
00:09:19.933 --> 00:09:25.701
CDIAC's public Elect>Ed summaries and outline informed broad debt-governance questions.

119
00:09:25.701 --> 00:09:30.341
No account-gated Elect>Ed lesson was used or represented as extracted.

120
00:09:30.341 --> 00:09:53.344
The capstone adapts general concepts concerning revenues, expenditures, financial management, assets, external resources, capital planning, partnerships, creditworthiness, and performance from Municipal Finances: A Handbook for Local Governments, edited by Catherine Farvacque-Vitkovic and Mihaly Kopanyi, World Bank, 2014, licensed CC BY 3.0 IGO.

121
00:09:53.344 --> 00:10:05.012
Changes include an original Torrance scenario, California-specific cautions, a ten-section decision packet, explicit equity analysis, stress testing, and monitoring thresholds.

122
00:10:05.012 --> 00:10:08.194
The World Bank does not endorse this adaptation.

123
00:10:08.194 --> 00:10:31.595
Additional grounding comes from Institute for Local Government resources on California municipal revenues, financial policies, capital financing, debt, enterprise funds, and reserves, plus City of Torrance public operating budgets, Capital Improvement Plans, budget reviews, project dashboards, Transparent Torrance materials, agenda reports, and ACFRs.

124
00:10:31.595 --> 00:10:34.313
The capstone habit is simple to remember.

125
00:10:34.313 --> 00:10:35.837
Define the public need.

126
00:10:35.837 --> 00:10:37.561
Compare real alternatives.

127
00:10:37.561 --> 00:10:38.953
Count the whole life.

128
00:10:38.953 --> 00:10:41.008
Match resources to obligations.

129
00:10:41.008 --> 00:10:42.268
Expose uncertainty.

130
00:10:42.268 --> 00:10:44.058
Protect equity and service.

131
00:10:44.058 --> 00:10:45.317
Record the reasons.

132
00:10:45.317 --> 00:10:46.643
Monitor the promise.

133
00:10:46.643 --> 00:10:50.408
You now have a repeatable fiscal lens for city decisions.

134
00:10:50.408 --> 00:10:56.220
Use it with humility, current evidence, professional advice, and an open public process.
